<article class="prose">
  <h2 class="label kind"><a href="/projects/">Projects</a></h2>
  <h1>Home 3D <span class="status active">active</span></h1>
  <p class="meta">Started October 2026<span class="sep">·</span>updated 19 November 2026</p>

  <p>Given a street address, gather what's publicly known about the lot and the house (floor plans, listing photos, street and aerial imagery, city footprint and LiDAR heights) and assemble it into a 3D model a homeowner can point at and mark up for a remodel.</p>
  <p>Two uses drive it. <a class="stub" href="#">See it new</a>: the model never changes, it supplies camera paths and geometry so an image or video model can restyle the real rooms. <a class="stub" href="#">Change it</a>: the homeowner edits the model itself, and the edits become the contractor's scope with real quantities.</p>

  <h2 id="where-it-stands"><a class="anchor" href="#where-it-stands">#</a>Where it stands</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One test house runs end to end, from raw inputs to an editable model.</li>
    <li>The floor plan lands on the city footprint at 0.85 to 0.93 overlap per level. See <a href="/notes/plan-fit/">Fitting a floor plan to the footprint</a>.</li>
    <li>Next: a second house, so nothing is tuned to just one.</li>
  </ul>
</article>

